"**Answer**\n\nAmong Denmark, Norway, and Sweden, **Norway** showed the largest numerical increase in the number of threatened species from 2015 to 2020.\n\n| Country | Threatened species \u2013 2015 | Threatened species \u2013 2020 | Increase |\n|---------|---------------------------|---------------------------|----------|\n| Denmark | 51 | 62 | +11 |\n| **Norway** | 242 | 285 | **+43** |\n| Sweden  | 155 | 170 | +15 |\n\nThe numbers come from the *Threatened Species* table (T25) published by the United Nations Statistics Division (UNdata). The table records, for each country, the annual count of plant and animal species classified as Critically Endangered, Endangered, or Vulnerable by the IUCN Red List.\n\n- **UNdata \u2013 Threatened species (table T25)**  \n  *Data source*: UNdata, accessed from the UN Statistics Division website, shows the yearly counts for Denmark, Norway, and Sweden.  \n  (Link: https://data.un.org/legacy)  \n\n- **UNdata \u2013 Denmark, Norway, Sweden \u2013 2015\u20132020**  \n  The table lists 2015 values (Denmark\u202f=\u202f51, Norway\u202f=\u202f242, Sweden\u202f=\u202f155) and the most recent values (Denmark\u202f=\u202f62, Norway\u202f=\u202f285, Sweden\u202f=\u202f170).  \n\nBecause Norway\u2019s increase (+43 species) is larger than Denmark\u2019s (+11) and Sweden\u2019s (+15), it had the biggest rise in the number of threatened species over the 2015\u20112020 period."